            <ArticleTitle>Syndrome of fascial incarceration of the long thoracic nerve: winged scapula.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>573-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.rboe.2015.08.011</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze the results from early intervention surgery in patients with the syndrome of fascial incarceration of the long thoracic nerve and consequent winged scapula.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Six patients with a syndrome of nerve trapping without specific nerve strain limitations were followed up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patients achieved improvement of their symptoms 6-20 months after the procedure. The motor symptoms completely disappeared, without any persistent pain. The medial deformity of the winged scapula improved in all cases, without any residual esthetic disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The approach of early surgical release seems to be a better predictor for recovery from non-traumatic paralysis of the anterior serratus muscle.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
